<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Statistical anomaly analysis</title>
<style>{{ font_css|safe }}body{font-family:'Source Code Pro',sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.6;color:#202f3b;max-width:950px;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem}h1,h2{color:#225977}section{margin:3rem 0;border-top:1px solid #cad7dd;padding-top:1rem}img{max-width:100%}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border-bottom:1px solid #dce3e8;padding:.4rem;text-align:left;overflow-wrap:anywhere}td small{display:block;color:#527385;margin-top:.25rem}.note{background:#eaf2f5;padding:1rem}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere}</style></head><body>
<h1>Statistical anomaly analysis</h1><p>{{ metadata.dataset_name }} · {{ metadata.timestamp }}</p>
<p class="note">This report shows patterns worth reviewing, not proof of wrongdoing. A higher score means a record looks more unusual <em>within this dataset</em>; it is not a probability.</p>
<h2>Overall anomaly index: {{ index if index is not none else 'Not available' }}{% if index is not none %} / 100{% endif %}</h2>
<p>The index combines the share of records marked for review, the size of observed differences, and how unusual those differences are under the chosen references. It is a summary for comparison, not a verdict.</p>
{% if components %}<ul><li>Records above the review score: {{ '%.1f'|format(components.review_share * 100) }}%</li><li>Combined size of differences: {{ '%.2f'|format(components.normalized_effect) }} on a 0–1 scale</li><li>Combined reference-test signal: {{ '%.2f'|format(components.adjusted_significance) }} on a 0–1 scale</li></ul>{% endif %}
{% if scoring_warning %}<p class="note">Anomaly scoring not available: {{ scoring_warning }}</p>{% endif %}
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ cover }}" alt="Analysis dashboard">
<h2>Main points</h2>{% for item in summary %}<p><strong>{{ item.severity|capitalize }}:</strong> {{ item.statement }}</p>{% endfor %}
<section><h2>About the dataset</h2><p>{{ profile.rows }} rows and {{ profile.columns }} columns. There is no fixed minimum number of rows. When fewer values are available, results are less precise; a narrow value range can also make digit patterns less informative.</p>
{% for name, col in profile.column_profiles.items() %}<p><strong>{{ name }}</strong>: {{ col.finite_count }} usable numbers ({{ col.positive_count }} positive), {{ col.missing }} missing. Values range from {{ col.finite_min }} to {{ col.finite_max }}; positive values range from {{ col.positive_min }} to {{ col.positive_max }} ({{ col.positive_orders_span }} powers of ten). The approximate sampling-uncertainty guide is ±{{ col.ecdf_95_band }}. Digit check: {{ col.benford }}{% if col.benford_reasons %} — {{ col.benford_reasons|join('; ') }}{% endif %}. Reference comparison: {{ col.continuous }}{% if col.continuous_reasons %} — {{ col.continuous_reasons|join('; ') }}{% endif %}.</p>{% endfor %}</section>
{% for section in sections %}<section><h2>{{ section.title }}</h2>
<h3>NUMBERS — What we measured</h3><p>A p-value describes how surprising a result would be if the chosen reference were a good match. It does not measure the chance of wrongdoing. The size of the difference tells you how far the data are from that reference.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Measure and meaning</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for label,value,description in section.number_rows %}<tr><td>{{ label }}<small>{{ description }}</small></td><td>{{ value }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>
{% if section.numbers.bins is defined %}<h4>Digit-by-digit detail</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Digit</th><th>Seen in data</th><th>Expected</th><th>Standardized difference</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for digit in section.numbers.bins %}<tr><td>{{ digit }}</td><td>{{ '%.2f'|format(section.numbers.observed[loop.index0] * 100) }}%</td><td>{{ '%.2f'|format(section.numbers.expected[loop.index0] * 100) }}%</td><td>{{ '%.2f'|format(section.numbers.z_scores[loop.index0]) }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>{% endif %}
<h3>CHART — What the pictures show</h3>{% for name,image,caption in section.charts %}<figure><img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ image }}" alt="{{ name }} chart"><figcaption>How to read this: {{ caption }}</figcaption></figure>{% endfor %}
<h3>STATISTICAL READING — What this may mean</h3>{% for text in section.reading %}<p>{{ text }}</p>{% endfor %}{% for text in section.caveats %}<p><strong>Keep in mind:</strong> {{ text }}</p>{% endfor %}{% for text in section.warnings %}<p><strong>Data note:</strong> {{ text }}</p>{% endfor %}</section>{% endfor %}
<section><h2>Rows to review</h2><p>These are row positions starting at zero in the uploaded data: {{ flagged_rows }}. A marked row may have an ordinary explanation.</p></section>
<section><h2>How to interpret this report</h2><p>Digit checks compare the share of each digit with a reference pattern. Distribution checks compare the shape of all values with a chosen reference. A relationship check compares observed values with model predictions. The scoring model ranks records within this dataset; it does not assign probabilities or identify wrongdoing. Smaller samples and repeated or rounded values can affect the results.</p>
<details><summary>Technical methods and thresholds</summary><p>Benford chart bands are per digit, using expected share ± the normal critical value for the selected band times the binomial standard error, with a half-count continuity adjustment. A 40% band is narrower than a 95% band; changing it does not change test results, Findings, or row flags. It is not a simultaneous confidence interval across all digits. For n independent finite observations the conservative 95% Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz ECDF bound is min(1, sqrt(log(40)/(2n))); it is an uncertainty guide, not a fitted-reference p-value. Positive-value span is log10(max) − log10(min). Benford chi-square results are warned if expected bin counts are below five; Benford MAD uses Nigrini's descriptive thresholds. KS D and Kuiper V use descriptive scales of 0.2 and 0.3, not universal critical values. P-values use the asymptotic or Monte Carlo method shown in each table and are adjusted across tests using Benjamini–Hochberg. The index is 100 × (0.2 × review share + 0.5 × normalized distribution difference + 0.3 × adjusted reference-test signal).</p></details></section>
<section><h2>How to reproduce this run</h2><pre>{{ metadata }}</pre></section></body></html>
